00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:02.310 align:start position:0%
 
hello you're watching the daily climate  show on sky news  on today's programme the country is  falling behind in the race against  climate  we speak to the cop 26 president taking  the fight to turkey  concerns drought could be the next  global pandemic  and the uk and europe join the race for  gigafactories  building car batteries  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show  where we track the changes that are  happening to our world right now  and we meet those coming up with the  solutions and climate change  is a global fight with real worldwide  consequences  the role of cop 26 in glasgow later this  year  is to encourage ambitious government  policies and in its words  accelerate action its president alok  sharma has traveled to turkey to  strengthen support  ahead of the climate conference now  greenhouse gas emissions in  turkey rose by 134 percent between 1990  and 2017 and they're expected  to go up again by between 40 and 70  percent by 2030. that's compared  to today's levels well turkey is taking  action on climate  in the paris agreement it states it's  bringing down carbon emissions to help  in the attempt to limit global warming  but it's described as critically  insufficient according  to climate action tracker and is one of  only  six countries not to have ratified the  deal  earlier i spoke to umet shahin a senior  scholar and the coordinator of  climate change studies at istanbul  policy center  everything turkey is doing to combat  climate changes  about national policies but  unfortunately turkey is not one of the  countries  to accept net zero target yet  2050 net zero targets  and turkey does not have any uh absolute  emissions reduction commitment well i'm  pleased to say that  cop 26 president alok sharma joins  me now mr sharma good to talk to you  when you see these images in turkey you  see an  urgent need to get them on board how  will you persuade them  well i've had a very good set of  discussions with a range of ministers  today  and there is a clear recognition here  that actually climate change is an  issue that affects all countries uh  climate change doesn't recognize borders  and turkey itself of course is a climate  vulnerable country it sits in the  mediterranean  basin which is one of the uh most  climate vulnerable regions in the world  the discussions i've had today have  centered around  how turkey can do even more and while  we're trying to pressure other countries  to do  their bit we need to get our own house  in order don't we a report out this week  says the uk  is woefully unprepared and more so than  five years ago for climate change  well i would say uh we should be judged  on uh what we have achieved  so since the year 2000 we have been the  country of all the g20 nations which has  decarbonized our economy the fastest  uh we're of course the first country to  put into law that we would be net zero  by 2050  and we've also set out a 2030 emission  reduction target  of 68 uh in terms of our emissions  and of course we are taking concrete  action uh at the end of last year  the prime minister put out his uh  10-point plan for a green industrial  revolution  demonstrating how we ourselves are going  to be transitioning to clean energies  and ensuring that we aren't able to meet  the environmental commitments that we  have made  mr sharma i would like to refer to our  data dashboard here which shows that  50 of the power mix is still coming  from fossil fuels when you look at that  you wonder  why are we opening coal mines well uh  if you are referring to uh the proposed  coal mine in in cumbria that of course  has now  gone to public inquiry so we'll see what  happens  as part of that but if you look at the  energy mix of the uk  40 percent of our energy was coming from  our electricity  power was coming from coal uh in 2012  we're now below two percent  and we've said by 2024 no more coal  in our electricity mix that is real  progress and the reason samantha we've  been able to do that  is we have grown our offshore wind  sector to now be the biggest in the  world  the largest offshore wind sector in the  world and we're going to quadruple that  over the next 10 years okay alex sharma  good to talk to you thank you  thank you well let's bring you some more  of the day's climate news now  and more than a quarter of uk schools  are located in areas above  the world health organization air  pollution limits that's according to new  data that's been released by the  campaign group  global action plan it suggests over 3.4  million children are learning  in an unhealthy environment  china has launched a spacecraft carrying  three astronauts  to part of its new space station the  country's first crewed mission  in five years the crew will spend three  months aboard the space station  236 miles above the earth  it comes after nasa criticized china for  failing to take responsibility for their  space debris  which impacts the environment  and an attempt to break the record for  the fastest train journey between  london and glasgow the avanti west coast  service arrived at glasgow central  just 21 seconds behind the current  record  despite not making a new record the  transport secretary grant chaps  says the attempt highlights how trains  are a clean  green way of connecting major cities in  the uk  now i want to bring in our data  dashboard for you here  well this shows the level of human  induced global warming since the  industrial revolution  just over 1.2 degrees and it's ticking  up the whole time  and this is the total amount of global  co2  emissions 2.41 trillion  tons and again it's ticking up even  while we look at it  well those rising temperatures are being  blamed for severe drought which is being  felt  all over the world so what is drought  let's have a look  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  well the un has released a report today  with a warning  that drought could become the next  global pandemic  mommy's is a un special representative  of the secretary general  for disaster risk reduction and joins me  now uh hello mommy how is drought  going to look like the next pandemic so  drought is a disaster which  expands across nations regions  the spatial scale is enormous and the  time scale  it can affect countries for years  if not decades thirdly the cascading  impact beyond the first  water stress the first impact is  is across sectors across  scales and then finally if a drought  happens  in one or two of the major production  areas of the four crops rice wheat  maize and soy at the same time it can  really ignite  a global food insecurity so that's why  we think that  drought could be the next pandemic  a pandemic without a vaccine of course  um  is it affecting more and more countries  absolutely so our report is drawing  um data from ipcc and  uh it says that in all of americas from  north  central to south australia africa  southern europe and central asia there  is  a high risk of severe drought because of  global warming  okay mommy misery really good to talk to  you thank you  thank you now west africa has been  greatly  impacted by drought but now there's a  campaign to plant  around 355 000 trees in 90 villages  across mali and senegal the project is  being  run by the international olympic  committee to offset its climate  emissions it's called the great green  wall initiative  and is in conjunction with the un and  tree aid  which aims to restore degraded  landscapes in  the sahel region in africa  and staying in that region the tree  planting efforts don't stop there we can  take you now  to ethiopia's matema forest  [Music]  trade works in dry land african  countries where  people are living on just on the front  line of the climate crisis areas  [Music]  i can tell you about the reserve because  of the climate change  he has lost some of the fertility of his  land  the rain is not no more reliable so some  of the crops he has been growing  as now not able to be grown and then  able to harvest good things  [Music]  the changing climates made his farming  no more enough to produce for his family  to feed the whole year  poor people living in there if they lose  their  security of producing and living in that  area  then after 20 years that  place will not be able to support  the peoples living around  there were some rivers which have been  flowing  year round we need to have some  support to cross those rivers because  they have water flowing during even dry  season  but nowadays because of this climate  christ those rivers  and streams which are no more there  now we know that one of the best ways to  decarbonize our transport system right  now  is by switching to electric cars in 2018  the uk produced half of all electric  cars in europe  but the expansion of gigafactories on  the continent  means that that could fall to just four  percent by 2030.  now is the uk racing to catch up  gigafactory ultimately is a factory that  produces batteries  or battery cells to be precise at scale  [Music]  it produces at least a gigawatt hour of  those batteries  if you use your dishwasher you would be  using around one  kilowatt hours of electricity and in  this case one gigawatt hour is at least  one million times that  we have seen a number of investments  happening in europe and if they all  scale up on time  in fact by 2030 europe will be producing  one third of the global battery  production capacity  the factories so far announced in the uk  a very small fraction  electric cars can be cheaper as petrol  cars  as soon as 2026 across europe  to make that happen we need to push car  makers  to invest in mass production of those  vehicles  well that is all for today tomorrow sky  news is in south sudan where  entire communities have been devastated  by severe flooding  that's at the same time tomorrow here on  sky news hope to see you then

